<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tyre</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tyre is an ancient Phoenician city on the coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, once an island stronghold and a great maritime trading power. Tyrian colonists founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>. Tyre resisted a long siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a> and was captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 332 BCE, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> period it was an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> port.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tyre.jpg" alt="Tyre" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RomanDeckert · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SubmergedEgyptianHarbour_TyreSour_Lebanon_RomanDeckert04112019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Syria-Palestine corridor was one of the most commercially vital regions of the medieval world, connecting the overland routes from Iraq, Persia and Central Asia to the Mediterranean ports of Acre, Beirut and <b>Tyre</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Iron Age, Byblos lacked the fleets of <b>Tyre</b> and Sidon and generally submitted to the empires that dominated the Levant rather than resisting them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The thirteen-year siege of the island city of <b>Tyre</b>, begun around 585 BCE, showed both the empire's strategic ambition and the limits of its siege capability against a maritime target, ending in a negotiated submission rather than a sack.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tyre</b> fell in 1124 under his successor Baldwin II, and Ascalon, the last Fatimid outpost on the coast, in 1153.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His conquest of the Achaemenid Persian Empire included victories at the Granicus (334 BCE), Issus (333 BCE) and Gaugamela (331 BCE), the sieges of <b>Tyre</b> and Gaza (332 BCE), and the defeat of Porus at the Hydaspes (326 BCE), demonstrating ingenuity in both pitched battle and siege warfare.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Punic Empire, more precisely the Carthaginian Empire, its name deriving from the Latin Punicus, “Phoenician”, originated as a colony of the Phoenician city of <b>Tyre</b> on the North African coast, traditionally founded in 814 BCE by the Tyrian princess Elissa, known to later tradition as Dido.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
